+# Merge review and verification
+
+## Baseline
+
+Prepared from the supplied `ZabyZach-main(1).zip`, whose ZIP comment identifies commit `682326ae7be7b7a12fc87357d831a27623e6330f`, and the updated ERP source at commit `fd7a15231d0bdbc4f3a76d0316ca3ea1845534b8`. This is a comparison against that supplied snapshot, not a claim that GitHub main has not advanced since it was downloaded. Compare the current branch before merging.
+
+The supplied Supabase column exports and function/policy/constraint export were read only. All 12 exported function bodies matched the migration versions in the uploaded ZIP after whitespace normalization. This does not verify database execution, function grants, triggers, RLS-enabled flags, or the completeness of production data.
+
+## Preserved
+
+`migration-review/protected-hashes.json` records the uploaded versions of 15 protected files: package and lock file, Supabase client and Vite environment declarations, index.html, Vite GitHub Pages base path, both existing workflow files, setup notes, and all six original SQL migrations. Automated checks require these files to remain byte-for-byte unchanged.
+
+The original auth-session/profile loading code, password sign-in, sign-out, and customer notification toast remain in App.tsx. The Inventory, Transfers & Counts, and Production screens and data loaders were isolated into ExistingDatabaseScreens.tsx. Their original three loaders and five transaction RPC call sites are checked for preservation. The newly supplied POS code (including record_sale and its concurrency/uncertain-confirmation protections), receipt type guards, and complete SupabaseReturnsPage are preserved as source snapshots and checked. The only POS edit formats phone entry as requested; the check reverses that one UI edit before comparing the full POS source byte-for-byte. Navigation routes still use the connected screens. Their existing behavior has not been redesigned or silently replaced by browser-only screens. The original per-catalog label printer remains available in Inventory.
+
+## Added/updated
+
+- Compact overlay navigation; manager/cashier-only Pizza Guide.
+- Checkout/phone formatting, pickup-time handling, stock-blocking and friendly messages, customer privacy, and sales/profit display improvements.
+- Supplier editing, purchasing workspace, inventory ordering review, history-derived scorecards, manual management reviews, corrections, visible short-shipment impact.
+- Searchable/printable barcode catalog, CSV/label exports, Code 128 checksum and quiet zones, SKU/barcode PO lookup.
+- The new barcode screen reads the current Supabase catalog/supplier assignments and POs instead of assuming the browser catalog equals the live database. It changes no records. Its catalog reads have not been exercised against the live project.
+- Existing browser data and item costs are backed up once before local upgrades under `zbyzack-erp-v1-before-erp-updates` and `zbyzack-erp-v1-costs-before-erp-updates`. Those backups are not copied to Supabase. Export them separately before any later production deployment if local history must be retained.
+- A new review-only GitHub workflow runs on erp-updates-current/PRs. It has read-only repository permissions and does not deploy or apply SQL.
+
+## Actual verification
+
+The local environment cannot download npm packages: both `npm ci` and a direct Supabase package lookup returned registry HTTP 403. Therefore a real dependency installation and production build with the actual Supabase library could not be completed here.
+
+Source TypeScript checks used the already-installed React/Vite tooling and a temporary, local-only Supabase compatibility declaration. That check catches application/module/type wiring errors but does not validate the actual Supabase library's types or behavior. The temporary declaration/runtime stub is not shipped. No mocked bundle is represented as a deployable production build, and no compiled assets are included.
+
+`node tests/run-all.mjs` passed locally: current domain/workflow tests, original checkout and POS-rewards tests, barcode encoding, receipt/accounting effects, EOQ, scorecards, source preservation, and browser-data backup. These tests do not authenticate or make network calls to Supabase.
+
+One original catalog test expected exactly 96 newly introduced drinks after migration. It fails against the new sample-history feature because recorded demo sales subsequently consume that stock (52 in the observed run). The original file is retained unchanged in `tests/original/`; its fixed opening-balance assumption is superseded by `tests/catalog-assortment.mjs` and `tests/availability-and-sample.mjs`, which check catalog migration, inventory consumption, history, persistence, and no silent restocking. It is not silently edited or counted as passing. The runner executes the other two original suites as well as all current suites. A new team-supabase-checkout test executes the actual new POS handler against mocked transport and checks server posting/receipt, no local-sale fallback, duplicate submission locking, unsupported feature guards, mapped customers, explicit failures, and uncertain confirmation protection. This does not prove live database behavior.
+
+Supported interactive browser QA was unavailable. Real auth, live writes, RLS enforcement, scanner hardware, responsive appearance, and cross-device synchronization have not been validated in this package. The real build remains gated by the new GitHub review workflow.
+
+## Remaining database integration — before main
+
+The existing project already mixed database screens and browser state. This code merge preserves that boundary and does not pretend to complete it. A browser ONLINE CUSTOMER sale or PO receipt currently does NOT change Supabase inventory; database production/transfers do NOT automatically update browser reports. Supplier screen changes currently do NOT edit the Supabase suppliers table. These discrepancies make this package unsuitable for live operations until the adapter work is completed.
+
+Next implementation must map suppliers/items/locations and historical records to database UUIDs, use validated transactional server functions for every receipt/sale/payment/order, implement customer ownership and role restrictions, and load reports/scorecards from the same records. Purchasing needs receipt-level history, payment transactions, PO promise snapshots, final-short/closed states, management reviews, and correction history. POS already posts through record_sale; online customer checkout still needs shared sale/order/payment/pickup metadata and safe idempotent submission, including recipe customization and rewards. Reporting must account for the preserved returns tables. Do not bulk-import generated history over the group's data.
+
+The existing receive_purchase_order function marks the PO Received for every call and uses the PO destination, so it cannot simply be reused unchanged for all the newer partial/final/chosen-location receipt behavior. No replacement function is applied by this package.
+
+Before a production merge, obtain a current repository comparison and a proper database backup, validate new migrations on an isolated database, and test role permissions plus two-device sale → inventory → finance and PO receipt → stock → invoice → payment → scorecard flows. A GitHub branch does not isolate the database. Keep the existing main deployment settings and environment values unchanged until the integration is verified.
+
+## V2: newer main work preserved
+
+The fresh archive changed only src/App.tsx relative to the first GitHub ZIP. It adds database-backed POS checkout and a connected returns/exchanges frontend. V2 retains both rather than reverting to the earlier local-only implementations. Existing main SQL and workflow files are still unchanged. The live Supabase function bodies were not re-exported for this update because no migration file changed in the fresh ZIP; no claim is made that the live database itself could not have changed independently.
+
+The updated POS intentionally retains the team's current restrictions: server checkout rejects custom modifiers and rewards redemption; a phone requires a visible Supabase customer mapping. New offline demo features do not bypass those guards. Online customer checkout, supplier/purchasing tools, scorecards, pickup queues, and browser reports still have separate local data. Live POS sales and returns therefore are not automatically reflected in those local reports. This remains a review-only code transfer, not a finished unified backend integration.
+
+Use a new erp-updates-current branch from current main so its history includes the newer team commits. Do not use the older package. The review workflow fetches main and compares its SHA to migration-review/baseline.json; it fails if newer main work appears, requiring another comparison. It does not push, deploy, apply SQL, or approve a merge.
